  "query_preview": "(This evening through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 249 PM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nThe Ohio Valley still remains beneath the influence of a strong area \nof high pressure over the SE CONUS. That said, troughing and \ncontinued CVA over the Upper Mississippi Vally this week will work \nto break down the ridge resulting in a slowly progressing long wave \npattern throughout the long term.  In the meantime, broad subsidence \nand surface high pressure to the east will keep conditions quiet \nthrough the work week. \n\nWinds will remain weak today, but starting tomorrow, slight \nincreases in the pressure gradient due to pressure depletion over \nthe Plains and Upper Midwest will allow for a more consistent light \nbreeze out of the S/SW. This should start to mitigate the diurnal \nfog development we've been experiencing over the last few nights. \nOn Thursday, there should be enough mid to low level flow and an \nincrease in PBL mixing for occasional gusts of 13-17kt during the \nafternoon. This consistent SW flow will also provide a slight \nwarming trend with afternoon highs approaching 80 by Thursday. \n\nEnsemble guidance is starting to pick up on the development of a \nmodest 850-700mb low late Friday through Sunday passing through the \nOhio Valley late Saturday into Sunday. While uncertainty on \nmagnitude and placement is still high, there is enough mid level \nmoisture return to warrant increase PoPs for Saturday night. Cannot \nrule out rain for Saturday evening, but there should be enough \nantecedent dry air to evaporate initial rainfall attempts holding off \nmeasurable rain until well into Saturday night. \n\nAs the ridge breaks down late this week through the weekend, a more \nprogressive longwave pattern is expected to develop. This will \nlikely place deeper troughing over the central CONUS by early next \nweek with increased chances for precipitation and larger temperature \nfluctuations likely for central Indiana to begin November.",
